Every flush, whether for a pause or a stop, armed a timer that tore
the AirPlay sessions down ten seconds later. A HomePod whose sender has
gone treats its next tap as "play something" and starts the household's
own library, and the sender's resume then finds the speaker busy. Now
only a stop arms it: a pause keeps the session, the HomePod's tap comes
back over the event channel as a play command, and resume restarts the
stream instead of reconnecting. The event bridge stops the engine when
Spotify stops or disconnects, so the sessions still end when there is
nothing left to play.

And a pipe input asked to start at a position answered 0, so a paused
pipe reported 0:00 until it was read again. It answers with the
position it was given: the writer's metadata is what set it.

--- a/src/player.c
+++ b/src/player.c
@@ -1589,7 +1589,14 @@
 	input_buffer_full_cb(player_playback_start);
     }
 
-  outputs_device_stop_delayed(device, device_streaming_cb);
+  // Only a stop lets the device go, after the timeout. A pause keeps the
+  // session: the device stays ours, its own controls still reach us, and
+  // resuming is a restart of the stream rather than a fresh connect.
+  // playback_stop() sets PLAY_STOPPED before the flush callbacks arrive;
+  // a pause sets PLAY_PAUSED after them, and a suspend sets it before,
+  // so neither is PLAY_STOPPED here.
+  if (player_state == PLAY_STOPPED)
+    outputs_device_stop_delayed(device, device_streaming_cb);
 
  out:
   commands_exec_end(cmdbase, 0);
--- a/src/inputs/pipe.c
+++ b/src/inputs/pipe.c
@@ -1053,7 +1053,17 @@
   return 0;
 }
 
+// A pipe cannot seek, but the writer on the other end can, and it reports
+// where it stands through the metadata pipe. When the player restarts this
+// input at a position (a pause does that), the position it names is the
+// one to count from; answering 0 here made every paused pipe read 0:00.
 static int
+seek(struct input_source *source, int seek_ms)
+{
+  return seek_ms;
+}
+
+static int
 stop(struct input_source *source)
 {
   struct pipe *pipe = source->input_ctx;
@@ -1187,6 +1197,7 @@
   .setup = setup,
   .play = play,
   .stop = stop,
+  .seek = seek,
   .metadata_get = metadata_get,
   .init = init,
   .deinit = deinit,
